# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file must never hold a
# real seed, password, or token.
#
# The server loads `.env` at startup (`import "dotenv/config"` in
# src/server/index.ts) and the client build loads it too (webpack.common.js).
# A real environment variable always wins over a line in this file, so
# `KEI_NODE=... npm run server-start` overrides what is set here.


# ── The economy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The issuer's seed: 64 hex characters. THIS IS THE ECONOMY — whoever holds it
# can mint this world's currency without limit. Generate one per deployment and
# keep it out of the repository, out of logs, and out of the client.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# This is a hard startup requirement for testnet, mainnet, and every custom
# KEI_NODE. The server refuses before opening its database or chain if it is
# missing or invalid. Only `KEI_NETWORK=mock` with no KEI_NODE may generate an
# ephemeral per-run seed, because that in-process ledger dies at the same time.
# Restoring a world means restoring this exact seed; replacing it changes every
# asset id and makes the old player property invisible to the new catalogue.
KEI_GAME_SEED=

# Which chain this world settles on: `testnet` (the default), `mainnet`, or
# `mock`.
#
#   testnet  the public M3 network at https://testnet.keicoin.org/rpc. Kei on it
#            is worth nothing and the chain may reset, but it is a real network:
#            a player's wallet outlives your server, which is the entire point of
#            the template.
#   mainnet  not open yet (SPEC §15). Selecting it without a KEI_NODE stops the
#            server with an explanation rather than settling somewhere else. It
#            also has no faucet, so this world's issuer address has to be funded
#            by a person before the first run.
#   mock     an in-process chain served at `/rpc`. No network, nothing worth
#            anything, and it dies with the process. Right for offline work.
KEI_NETWORK=testnet

# URL of the Kei node to settle against, overriding the public one for whichever
# KEI_NETWORK is selected. Unset is the normal case. Any custom node is treated
# as persistent and therefore requires KEI_GAME_SEED, even with KEI_NETWORK=mock.
KEI_NODE=

# `off` disables paying Kei for gold. SPEC §8 requires the game to be playable
# with payments switched off. Any other value (or unset) leaves the exchange on.
KEI_EXCHANGE=

# `on` lets the reward outbox actually mint. Anything else (or unset) runs it in
# dry-run: the queue, the reconciler and the retention sweep all work and log what
# they would have paid, and nothing is signed.
#
# Off by default on purpose. Nothing enqueues into the outbox yet, so today it
# changes nothing either way — but the order this is meant to be turned on in is
# ship the workers, watch them make no chain writes, make that version the
# rollback floor, and only then enable it in a separately reviewed deployment
# (issue #9). Not in the same deployment as wallet proof.
KEI_REWARD_DELIVERY=

# What a new character claims once from `POST /kei/purse`, in gold. Unset is 500,
# which buys the vendor's sword at 100 and two small potions at 150 each.
#
# This is a mint the issuer signs, bounded by the game's own login token and by a
# durable one-per-address, one-per-character record — not by anything
# cryptographic, because this server still cannot prove a wallet belongs to the
# player holding it. `0` hands out nothing at all, which is the right setting for
# a deployment that would rather its players earned their first gold. A value
# that is not a whole number between 0 and 10000 stops the server rather than
# being rounded into something nobody chose.
KEI_STARTING_GOLD=


# ── The database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Accounts, characters, and where they were standing. Not money — that is on the
# chain. Which engine runs is `database` in src/shared/Config.ts ("sqllite" or
# "mysql"), not an environment variable; only the connection details are here.

# sqlite (the default engine). Path to the database file, relative to the
# working directory the server was started from.
DATABASE_PATH=./database.db

# mysql. Read only when Config.database is "mysql".
DATABASE_HOST=localhost
DATABASE_DB=t5c
DATABASE_USER=root
DATABASE_PASSWORD=


# ── Runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `production` closes `/kei/grant` (the development faucet, which mints gold to
# any address on request), never loads the Colyseus monitor, and turns off the
# 250ms latency simulation. Anything else is treated as development.
#
# `/kei/purse` stays open in production and is what a new player actually claims:
# see KEI_STARTING_GOLD above.
NODE_ENV=development

# The listen port is `port` in src/shared/Config.ts (3000), not an environment
# variable. A host that assigns a port expects that file to be edited.

# Which pages a browser may let call this server, comma separated. An origin is
# scheme://host[:port] with no path and no trailing slash, because that is what
# a browser sends — anything else is refused at startup rather than silently
# matching nothing.
#
# Pages served by this server are always allowed and need no entry here. This is
# for the split deployment: keicoin.org serves the client, mmo.keicoin.org runs
# the rooms.
#
#   KEI_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://keicoin.org
#
# Empty means the webpack dev server (:8080) while NODE_ENV is not `production`,
# and nothing at all once it is. There is no `*`: that is the bug this replaced.
KEI_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=


# ── The client build ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the built client should look for the game server. Read by
# webpack.common.js at BUILD time and compiled into the bundle — changing it
# afterwards requires another `npm run client-build`.
#
# Empty means "wherever this page came from", which is what `npm run client-dev`
# wants and what breaks the moment the client is hosted somewhere the rooms are
# not.
#
#   GAME_SERVER=https://mmo.example.org npm run client-build
GAME_SERVER=


# ── Tests ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Base URL `npm run test:e2e` points at. Defaults to http://localhost:3000.
KEI_TEST_BASE=http://localhost:3000
